#Stochastic character mapping METHODS:
#Because no extant lineage exhibited a mixed state (0|1 or 1|0), chamber morphology and attachment-thread use were perfectly associated in our dataset. The primary analysis therefore mapped a single binary character (0|0 vs. 1|1) using an all-rates-different (ARD) model and a flat root prior, with 1000 stochastic maps.
    #Node posteriors were summarized with describe.simmap.
#To test whether collapsing the joint states influenced our inferences, we repeated the analysis using all four joint states (0|0, 0|1, 1|0, 1|1). 
#Transitions were restricted to single-trait changes (no simultaneous changes in both traits), matching the structure of the dependent model in our Pagel’s test, 
#and each permitted transition was given its own rate (8 free parameters;all rates differed). 
#Because two states have no observed tips, results from this model depend on the assumed root state, so we ran it under two root priors: 
#a flat prior (probability 0.25 per state) and a prior fixed at 0|0 (simple chamber, no attachment threads), reflecting an unmodified ancestral condition. 
#Each used 1000 stochastic maps. Models were compared with AIC.
